================================================================================
2. SOBOLEV NORMS & CAUCHY-SCHWARZ TRACE BOUNDS
================================================================================
Dirichlet kinetic energy ||T'_v||, curvature ||T''_v||, and Cauchy-Schwarz trace bounds:
    |D_1| <= sqrt(2) * kappa * ||T'_v||_2 * sqrt(sum_{m=1}^N m^2)

   N   ||T_v||_{L^2}    ||T_v'||_{L^2}   ||T_v''||_{L^2}      CS Bound Ratio
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
   8             1.0           2.77807           13.1781          2.44717e-8
  12             1.0           2.99621           15.2875         2.41934e-11
  16             1.0           3.11013           16.4351         7.50492e-14
  20             1.0           3.17849            17.138         4.37899e-16
  24             1.0            3.2174           17.5428          7.5837e-18
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Note: The elementary Cauchy–Schwarz bound is extremely non-sharp for D_1.
This provides evidence that signed cancellation is active, but because the bound
itself grows with N (scaling as ~ N^{3/2}), its ratio to D_1 becoming tiny is an
expected consequence of D_1 -> 0 rather than a separate proof of mechanism.
Furthermore, because ||T_N||_{L^2} = 1 while T_N(0) = D_0 -> 0, the endpoint value
is not controlled by the global L^2 norm in any useful asymptotic sense.
Endpoint suppression is encoded in the specific ground-state mode vector rather
than being a generic Sobolev phenomenon.

