Note: These scaling diagnostics are exploratory. With N in {16, 20, 24}, Diagnostic A
is a 3-point interpolation, while Diagnostics B and C have a single degree of information.
They serve as diagnostics to guide larger-N calculations rather than inferential proof.

Strategic observation: If Delta_N = -log|D_0| + log|D_1| behaves smoothly across N
while -log|D_0| and -log|D_1| grow rapidly, this confirms that the ratio D_0 / D_1
(the first-jet scale u_1) is the analytically tractable object to target for higher-N
calculations (e.g. N = 40, 60, 100).

